1. WHAT IS A PIPNODE PLUGIN?
----------------------------

Pipnode is a desktop visual flow editor: the user drags "nodes"
onto a worksheet and wires their outputs to the inputs of other
nodes to build a data-processing graph.  Each node type is a C
class derived from `PnNode` (a `GObject` subclass) and registered
with the process-wide `PnNodeFactory` so that the palette, the
JSON load path, and the worksheet renderer can find it by name.

A plugin is a shared object (`.so` on Linux, `.dylib` on macOS,
`.dll` on Windows) that contains one or more `PnNode` subclasses
and registers them with the factory at start-up.  From the user's
point of view a plugin-shipped node is indistinguishable from a
built-in: it appears in the palette, can be saved into a worksheet
JSON file, and round-trips through load and save just like every
other node.

Plugins exist so that:

  - third parties can ship node types without forking pipnode;
  - the project itself can deliver node types in separately
    versioned shared objects (the bundled `pipnode-network` plugin
    that ships Ping and DNS Check is exactly this);
  - downstream packagers can choose which optional node types to
    install on a given machine.


2. HOW DYNAMIC LOADING WORKS (BACKGROUND)
-----------------------------------------

If you have never written a dynamically loaded library, here is
the short version.

A regular executable is linked at build time against a fixed list
of shared libraries.  The list is recorded in the ELF header and
the dynamic linker (`/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2`) resolves them
when the program starts.  After that, no more code can join the
process unless the program explicitly asks for it.

The POSIX `dlopen(3)` family of calls is how a program asks for
more code at run time:

  - `dlopen("path/to/lib.so", RTLD_NOW)`         opens the file
  - `dlsym(handle, "symbol_name")`               looks up a symbol
  - `dlclose(handle)`                            unmaps the file

Pipnode does not call `dlopen` directly.  Instead it uses GLib's
portable wrapper, `GModule`, which speaks `dlopen` on POSIX,
`LoadLibrary` on Windows, and `NSAddImage` on older macOS -- same
contract, three backends.  The relevant calls are:

  - `g_module_open(path, flags)`        wraps `dlopen`
  - `g_module_symbol(mod, name, &fn)`   wraps `dlsym`
  - `g_module_make_resident(mod)`       pins the module for the
                                        rest of the process's life
                                        so it cannot be unloaded
  - `g_module_close(mod)`               wraps `dlclose`

The "make resident" step matters because pipnode registers
`GType` instances inside the plugin's `pn_plugin_init`, and GLib
does not support unregistering a `GType`.  If the plugin's `.so`
were ever unmapped while a node it registered was still in use,
the next call into the type's vtable would jump into freed
memory and crash the process.  Plugins are therefore a one-way
door: once loaded they stay loaded until the process exits.


3. THE PLUGIN ABI IN ONE PAGE
-----------------------------

The contract between pipnode and a plugin is defined in the
public header `<pipnode/pn-plugin.h>`, which itself pulls in
`<pipnode/pn-node-factory.h>` so the entry point can register
types with the factory.  There are exactly three pieces.

  (a) A constant, the ABI version:

        #define PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION 1

      Bumped whenever an incompatible change to `PnNode`,
      `PnNodeClass`, the factory registration contract, or the
      entry-point signature ships in a release.  Plugins stamp
      this value into the `PnPluginInfo` they return; the host
      refuses to load a plugin whose declared version differs.

  (b) An identity struct that the plugin returns:

        typedef struct
        {
            int          abi_version;
            const gchar *name;
            const gchar *version;
            const gchar *description;
        } PnPluginInfo;

      `name` is required and shows up in the inspector as the
      provenance string for every type the plugin registers.
      `version` is required and free-form ("1.0.0", "2025-04-12").
      `description` is optional.  All three string fields must
      have static lifetime: typically they are string literals
      inside a static const struct.

  (c) An entry-point function that the plugin must export under
      the symbol name "pn_plugin_init":

        typedef const PnPluginInfo *
        (*PnPluginInitFunc) (PnNodeFactory *factory);

      The host calls this once with the process-wide
      `PnNodeFactory`.  The plugin calls
      `pn_node_factory_register(factory, MY_TYPE_FOO)` for each
      `PnNode` subclass it provides and returns a borrowed
      pointer to a static `PnPluginInfo`.  Returning NULL is
      treated as a failed load.

That is the entire contract.  No vtable, no init/fini pair, no
context object -- one symbol, one struct, one constant.


4. WRITING A MINIMAL PLUGIN
---------------------------

A complete, working plugin fits in roughly fifty lines of C.
The file `tests/plugins/echo/pn-echo.c` in the source tree is
exactly such a plugin and is the easiest place to start reading.
A condensed version, with explanatory comments, follows.

    /*  my-echo.c  ---  pass-through demo node  */

    #include <gmodule.h>

    #include <pipnode/pn-node.h>
    #include <pipnode/pn-message.h>
    #include <pipnode/pn-node-factory.h>
    #include <pipnode/pn-plugin.h>

    /*  --- 1.  Declare the GType. --- */

    #define MY_TYPE_ECHO (my_echo_get_type ())

    G_DECLARE_FINAL_TYPE (MyEcho, my_echo, MY, ECHO, PnNode)

    struct _MyEcho
    {
        PnNode parent_instance;
    };

    G_DEFINE_TYPE (MyEcho, my_echo, PN_TYPE_NODE)

    /*  --- 2.  Override the receive vfunc. --- */

    static void
    my_echo_receive (PnNode *node, PnMessage *message)
    {
        /* Forward every incoming message unchanged. */
        pn_node_emit_message (node, message);
    }

    /*  --- 3.  Pin class-level metadata. --- */

    static void
    my_echo_class_init (MyEchoClass *klass)
    {
        PnNodeClass *nc = PN_NODE_CLASS (klass);

        nc->receive    = my_echo_receive;
        nc->class_name = "Echo";              /* palette label   */
        nc->icon       = "\xef\x81\xa4";      /* fa-share glyph  */
        nc->color      = (GdkRGBA){0.55, 0.65, 0.95, 1.0};
        nc->category   = "Filters";           /* palette group   */
        nc->has_input  = TRUE;
        nc->has_output = TRUE;
    }

    static void
    my_echo_init (MyEcho *self)
    {
        (void) self;   /* nothing to initialise per instance */
    }

    /*  --- 4.  Export the plugin entry point. --- */

    G_MODULE_EXPORT const PnPluginInfo *
    pn_plugin_init (PnNodeFactory *factory)
    {
        static const PnPluginInfo info = {
            .abi_version = PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION,
            .name        = "EchoPlugin",
            .version     = "1.0.0",
            .description = "Pass-through demo node.",
        };

        pn_node_factory_register (factory, MY_TYPE_ECHO);
        return &info;
    }

A few notes on the four blocks above.

(1) The `G_DECLARE_FINAL_TYPE` macro from GLib produces all the
    boilerplate for a non-derivable GObject subclass: the
    `MyEcho` instance struct, the `MyEchoClass` class struct, the
    `my_echo_get_type ()` accessor, the `MY_ECHO ()` /
    `MY_IS_ECHO ()` casts, and so on.  Use `G_DECLARE_DERIVABLE_TYPE`
    instead if your node type is meant to be subclassed.

(2) `receive` is the only `PnNodeClass` virtual method most node
    types need to override.  It runs whenever a wire delivers a
    message to this node's input port.  To emit a message on the
    output port (for downstream nodes to receive), call
    `pn_node_emit_message (node, message)`.

(3) The `class_name`, `icon`, `color`, `category`, `has_input`,
    and `has_output` fields are read by the palette and the
    worksheet renderer to draw your node.  They live on the
    class struct rather than the instance because every instance
    of `MyEcho` looks the same; pinning them once during
    `_class_init` saves an allocation per node.

(4) `G_MODULE_EXPORT` annotates the function so the linker keeps
    the symbol visible to `dlsym`.  On most ELF toolchains this
    is a no-op (symbols default to public), but on Windows it
    expands to `__declspec(dllexport)` and is therefore required
    for portability.  The function name must be exactly
    `pn_plugin_init` -- that is the symbol the host looks up, and
    the macro `PN_PLUGIN_INIT_SYMBOL` in `<pn-plugin.h>` is the
    single source of truth.

A plugin can register more than one type from a single
`pn_plugin_init`; the bundled network plugin registers `PnPing`
and `PnDns` from one entry point, for example.


4a. NODES WITH MORE THAN ONE INPUT
----------------------------------

Most nodes have a single input port, which the `has_input`
boolean turns on.  A node can instead expose several input ports
by calling `pn_node_set_n_inputs (node, N)` in its `_init` (this
also turns `has_input` on).  The worksheet then draws N input
tabs stacked down the node's left edge, and each tab can be wired
independently.

Because there is still one `receive` vfunc, a multi-input node
tells its ports apart by asking which one the current message
arrived on:

    static void
    my_blend_receive (PnNode *node, PnMessage *message)
    {
        gint input = pn_node_current_input ();   /* 0 .. N-1 */
        /* ... store/act on the message for that port ... */
    }

`pn_node_current_input ()` is valid only for the duration of the
`receive` call.  A node that must combine its inputs (rather than
react to each independently) typically latches the most recent
message per port and only acts once every port has one; the
bundled image plugin's `pn_image_node_process2 ()` helper does
exactly this for its two-input `Blend` node.

Connections to a specific input survive save/load: a wire to a
port other than 0 stores a `target_input` integer in the
`connections` array (omitted, and assumed 0, for the ordinary
single-input case).  Wires created in C address a port with
`pn_wire_new_full (source, target, target_input)`;
`pn_wire_new (source, target)` is the `target_input == 0` shortcut.


5. BUILDING THE PLUGIN: STANDALONE MAKEFILE
-------------------------------------------

The simplest possible out-of-tree build needs `pkg-config` to
locate pipnode's headers and link line, plus the standard
`-fPIC -shared` toolchain flags for a position-independent
shared object.  A minimal Makefile:

    CC          = cc
    CFLAGS     := -Wall -O2 -fPIC \
                  $(shell pkg-config --cflags gtk+-3.0 \
                                              json-glib-1.0 \
                                              gmodule-2.0 \
                                              pipnode 2>/dev/null)
    LDLIBS     := $(shell pkg-config --libs gtk+-3.0 \
                                            json-glib-1.0 \
                                            gmodule-2.0 \
                                            pipnode 2>/dev/null)

    my_echo.so: my-echo.c
            $(CC) $(CFLAGS) -shared -o $@ $< $(LDLIBS)

    install: my_echo.so
            install -D -m 0644 my_echo.so \
                    $(DESTDIR)$(HOME)/.local/share/pipnode/plugins/my_echo.so

The `pkg-config --cflags pipnode` invocation resolves once
pipnode has been `make install`-ed: the host's top-level
`Makefile.am` installs `pipnode.pc` into
`$(libdir)/pkgconfig/`, and `pkg-config` picks it up from there
without any further configuration.  In addition to the usual
`Cflags:` / `Libs:` / `Requires:` fields, the .pc file exposes
two pkg-config variables a plugin's build system can query to
avoid hard-coding install paths:

  - `plugindir` -- the directory the host scans for plugin .so
    files (typically `$libdir/pipnode/plugins`).  Plugin
    Makefile.am rules should install into this directory so the
    default plugin search path (section 7) picks them up.

  - `helpdir`   -- the flat directory the host's help browser
    resolves `<TypeName>.html` files from (typically
    `$pkgdatadir/help`).  Plugin Makefile.am rules that ship
    per-node HTML help files install into this directory.

Both variables come out of `pkg-config --variable=plugindir
pipnode` and `pkg-config --variable=helpdir pipnode`
respectively.

Three filename conventions are worth knowing:

  - The shared object's basename is what the user sees in error
    messages and what pipnode uses for filename-based dedup
    (section 9).  Pick something readable, e.g. `my_echo.so`,
    `acme_crypto.so`.

  - There is no `lib` prefix.  Plugins are loadable modules,
    not libraries you link against, so the `lib` prefix that
    `gcc -shared` uses by default would only confuse readers.
    With autotools, `-module -avoid-version` produces the right
    name; with a hand-written Makefile, just call your output
    file `my_echo.so` directly.

  - Avoid SONAME version suffixes (`.so.0.0.0` and friends).
    `g_module_open` will only find `my_echo.so`, not
    `my_echo.so.0`.


6. BUILDING THE PLUGIN: AUTOTOOLS (THE BUNDLED APPROACH)
--------------------------------------------------------

The bundled `plugins/network/` plugin in this source tree is
built with autotools and libtool.  The relevant excerpts from
`plugins/network/Makefile.am`, suitable as a template for any
new bundled plugin or for an out-of-tree autotools project:

    plugindir = $(pkglibdir)/plugins

    plugin_LTLIBRARIES = my_echo.la

    my_echo_la_SOURCES = my-echo.c

    my_echo_la_CFLAGS  = $(GTK_CFLAGS) \
                         $(JSON_GLIB_CFLAGS) \
                         $(GMODULE_CFLAGS) \
                         -I$(top_srcdir)/lib

    my_echo_la_LDFLAGS = -module -avoid-version -shared

    my_echo_la_LIBADD  = $(top_builddir)/lib/libpipnode.la \
                         $(GTK_LIBS) \
                         $(JSON_GLIB_LIBS) \
                         $(GMODULE_LIBS)

What each piece is doing:

  - `plugindir = $(pkglibdir)/plugins` declares the install
    directory.  `pkglibdir` is `$prefix/lib/pipnode` by default,
    so the plugin lands at `/usr/local/lib/pipnode/plugins/` on
    a typical install.  This is exactly the directory pipnode
    scans at start-up (section 7).

  - `plugin_LTLIBRARIES` (rather than `lib_LTLIBRARIES`) tells
    automake to install into `$plugindir`.

  - `-module` tells libtool that the result is a loadable module
    (no `lib` prefix, no requirement that every symbol be
    resolvable at link time).

  - `-avoid-version` drops the SONAME `.0.0.0` suffix so the
    installed file is a plain `my_echo.so`.

  - `-shared` forces the shared form even on platforms where
    libtool would otherwise also emit a static archive.

  - `LIBADD` against `libpipnode.la` makes the plugin link
    against the host library so symbols like
    `pn_node_factory_register` and `pn_node_emit_message`
    resolve at run time against pipnode itself rather than
    being inlined into the .so.

For a plugin that lives outside this source tree, replace
`-I$(top_srcdir)/lib` with `$(PIPNODE_CFLAGS)` and the libtool
`LIBADD` line with `$(PIPNODE_LIBS)`, both produced by
`PKG_CHECK_MODULES([PIPNODE], [pipnode])` in your own
`configure.ac`.

A small autoconf helper, `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_CHECK`, is installed
alongside `pipnode.pc` (into `$(datadir)/aclocal/`) so an
out-of-tree plugin's `configure.ac` can do the whole probe in
one line:

    PIPNODE_PLUGIN_CHECK([0.1.0])  dnl minimum pipnode version

In addition to running the equivalent `PKG_CHECK_MODULES` and
`AC_SUBST`-ing `PIPNODE_CFLAGS` / `PIPNODE_LIBS`, the macro also
pulls `plugindir` and `helpdir` out of `pipnode.pc` and exposes
them as `PIPNODE_PLUGINDIR` and `PIPNODE_HELPDIR` so the
plugin's `Makefile.am` can install into the directories the
host actually scans at run-time without hard-coding
`$(libdir)/pipnode/plugins` or `$(datadir)/pipnode/help`:

    plugindir   = $(PIPNODE_PLUGINDIR)
    plugin_LTLIBRARIES = my_echo.la
    ...

    pnhelpdir   = $(PIPNODE_HELPDIR)
    pnhelp_DATA = help/MyEcho.html

The macro requires that the system pipnode install is recent
enough to ship `pipnode.pc` -- the macro errors out with a
clear message if the .pc file is present but does not expose a
`plugindir` variable, which would mean pipnode predates the
helper-file work.


7. WHERE PIPNODE LOOKS FOR PLUGINS
-----------------------------------

At start-up both `pipnode` (the GUI) and `pipnode-run` (the
headless runner) call `pn_node_factory_load_plugins_default()`,
which scans the following directories in order.  The first
plugin to claim a given basename wins (section 9), so paths
listed earlier override paths listed later.

  1. Every directory in the `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH` environment
     variable.  Colon-separated on POSIX; pipnode honours the
     platform's native search-path separator
     (`G_SEARCHPATH_SEPARATOR_S`).

         PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH=/path/one:/path/two pipnode

     Useful for testing an in-development plugin against an
     installed pipnode without overwriting the installed copy.

  2. `$XDG_DATA_HOME/pipnode/plugins/`, with the canonical
     XDG fallback to `$HOME/.local/share/pipnode/plugins/`.
     The right place for a per-user install: drop a `.so` in
     here and the next pipnode start-up picks it up.

  3. `$pkglibdir/plugins/`, baked into pipnode at compile time
     via the `-DPKGLIBDIR=` macro.  Typically
     `/usr/local/lib/pipnode/plugins/` for a `./configure` with
     no `--prefix` override, or
     `/usr/lib/pipnode/plugins/` for a distribution package.
     This is where `make install` lands plugins built with the
     bundled-approach autotools rules from section 6.

  4. `<top_builddir>/plugins/*/.libs/`, a build-tree fallback
     used during development.  When pipnode is run straight
     from the source tree (without `make install`), this is
     how the bundled `pipnode-network` plugin becomes visible
     to `src/pipnode` and `src/pipnode-run` without anyone
     having to set `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH`.  An installed
     pipnode also has this directory baked into the binary,
     but the path almost certainly does not exist on the
     target machine, so the loader silently skips it.

A directory that does not exist (or that cannot be opened) is
treated as "no plugins here" and produces no warning.  An
unreadable plugin file inside an existing directory does
produce a warning but does not abort the rest of the scan.


8. HOW A PLUGIN IS LOADED AT RUN TIME
-------------------------------------

For each candidate `.so` file found in the search path, the
loader does the following in `pn_node_factory_load_plugin()`:

  1.  Canonicalises the path with `g_canonicalize_filename`
      (resolves symlinks and `..` segments) so two paths that
      reach the same file dedupe against each other.

  2.  Checks that neither the canonical path nor the file's
      basename has been seen before; if it has, returns success
      silently without re-opening the file.

  3.  Calls `g_module_open(path, G_MODULE_BIND_LAZY |
      G_MODULE_BIND_LOCAL)`.  Lazy binding means symbols inside
      the plugin are resolved on first use rather than on load,
      which is faster.  Local binding keeps the plugin's
      symbols out of the global symbol table -- two plugins can
      both define a static helper called `read_byte` without
      the second one's call accidentally jumping into the first
      one's copy.

  4.  Calls `g_module_symbol(mod, "pn_plugin_init", &fn)`.
      A missing symbol fails the load with
      `PN_NODE_FACTORY_PLUGIN_ERROR_NO_ENTRY`.

  5.  Calls `fn(factory)`.  This is where the plugin runs its
      `G_DEFINE_TYPE`-generated `_get_type` accessors and its
      `pn_node_factory_register` calls.

  6.  Validates the returned `PnPluginInfo`: a NULL return is a
      failed load; an `abi_version` mismatch is a failed load
      and the types the plugin already registered are popped
      back off the factory; an empty `name` is a failed load.

  7.  Backfills `PnPluginInfo.name` onto every type the plugin
      registered whose `PnNodeClass.plugin_name` still reads
      the inherited `"Internal"` default.  This is how the
      inspector ends up showing your plugin's identifier as
      the provenance for nodes that did not bother overriding
      `plugin_name` themselves.

  8.  Calls `g_module_make_resident` so the .so cannot be
      unmapped, and adds both the canonical path and the
      basename to the dedup tables.

  9.  Logs `pipnode: loaded plugin "<name>" <version> from
      <path>` at the GLib `INFO` level.


9. DEDUPLICATION: WHAT STOPS A PLUGIN FROM LOADING TWICE
--------------------------------------------------------

The loader maintains two hash sets, both keyed by string:

  - `loaded_paths`     -- canonical `g_canonicalize_filename`
                         paths.  Catches the same physical file
                         reached through two overlapping search
                         directories (e.g. a symlink farm or
                         two directories listed in
                         `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH`).

  - `loaded_basenames` -- `g_path_get_basename` of the same
                         paths.  Catches the case where two
                         physically distinct files ship the
                         same `GType` set: typically the
                         system-installed
                         `/usr/local/lib/pipnode/plugins/foo.so`
                         and the build-tree-fallback
                         `<top_builddir>/plugins/foo/.libs/foo.so`
                         are different inodes but register
                         identical types.

Without basename dedup the second registration would crash
with `cannot register existing type` because `GType` is a
process-global namespace.  Whichever directory was scanned
first wins; the search order above (env var, user, system,
build tree) is therefore also the precedence order for
overlapping plugins of the same name.


10. ABI VERSIONING
------------------

`PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION` is a single integer in
`<pn-plugin.h>`.  It is bumped whenever an incompatible change
to `PnNode`, `PnNodeClass`, the factory registration contract,
or the plugin entry-point signature ships in a release.

A plugin built against ABI version N exposes "N" in its
`PnPluginInfo.abi_version`.  The host compares this to its own
`PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION` and refuses to load on mismatch -- it
logs a warning and skips the plugin instead of letting a stale
.so in the user's plugin directory crash the process with a
silent layout mismatch.

What this means in practice:

  - Plugins are recompiled and redistributed against the new
    ABI when pipnode bumps it.  There is no compatibility
    shim layer.

  - A plugin that only ever needs to ship one node type and
    that uses no internal pipnode APIs is unlikely to be
    affected by ABI bumps in practice; the contract surface
    that the version protects is small.

  - If you maintain a plugin out-of-tree, treat the ABI
    version as a fast-failing match against pipnode's release
    notes: bump your plugin's version, recompile against the
    new headers, and ship.


11. INSPECTOR ATTRIBUTION
-------------------------

Every `PnNode` carries a `plugin_name` field on its class
struct, default `"Internal"`.  The factory shows this string
in the inspector pane and in the palette tooltip so the user
can tell where a given node type came from.

You can pin the value yourself in `_class_init`:

    nc->plugin_name = "EchoPlugin";

but you do not have to.  The loader backfills the value from
your `PnPluginInfo.name` for every type whose
`plugin_name` still equals the inherited `"Internal"` default,
so the common case (one plugin, several types, all attributed
to the same plugin) does not require a per-class override.


12. LIMITATIONS AND GOTCHAS
---------------------------

- *No unload.*  Once loaded, a plugin's .so stays mapped until
  the process exits.  Reloading a plugin during development
  requires restarting pipnode.

- *No partial loads.*  If your `pn_plugin_init` calls
  `pn_node_factory_register` ten times and then returns NULL,
  the loader pops the ten registrations off the factory but
  the underlying `GType` registrations stay in place -- they
  cannot be undone.  In practice this means you should validate
  whatever conditions your plugin needs (configuration files,
  external libraries) before the first
  `pn_node_factory_register`, not after.

- *GType names must be globally unique.*  Two plugins that both
  define a `MyEcho` GType cannot coexist in the same process;
  the second one's `g_type_register_static` will fail at the
  GLib level with `cannot register existing type`.  Pick a
  prefix that is unlikely to collide (e.g. your project's name
  or a registered DNS-style identifier).

- *Threading.*  Pipnode runs the GTK main loop on a single
  thread; node `receive` callbacks run on that thread.  If
  your plugin does anything blocking (network I/O, file
  system, `usleep`) it must do so from a worker thread and
  marshal results back to the main loop with
  `g_main_context_invoke`.  Look at how `PnPing` uses the
  worker-thread idiom for an idiomatic pattern.

- *Headless safety.*  `pipnode-run` does not link or
  initialise GTK widgets, but it does link `libpipnode` (which
  in turn is built against GTK).  Your plugin can safely call
  any GLib / GObject / GIO API in its node implementation; it
  must not call into `gtk_*` from inside `receive` or any
  other code path that runs under `pipnode-run`, since no GTK
  display has been opened.  Settings dialogs and other UI
  hooks must be guarded so they only run inside the GUI host.


13. DEBUGGING A PLUGIN THAT DOES NOT LOAD
-----------------------------------------

Start the binary with `G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all` to see every
message the loader emits:

    G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all pipnode

Then read the output for one of the four common failures.

(a) "failed to open plugin ...":
    `g_module_open` returned NULL.  The reason follows the
    colon -- usually a missing symbol from a transitive
    dependency.  Check with `ldd my_plugin.so` that every
    NEEDED entry resolves on the target system.

(b) "plugin ... does not export pn_plugin_init":
    The .so loaded but the entry-point symbol is absent or
    not visible.  Either you forgot the function, you spelled
    it wrong, or you forgot `G_MODULE_EXPORT` on a non-ELF
    toolchain.  `nm -D my_plugin.so | grep pn_plugin_init`
    should show a `T` entry; if it shows nothing or a `t` the
    symbol is private.

(c) "ABI version N does not match host ABI M":
    The .so was built against a different pipnode release.
    Recompile against the running pipnode's headers.

(d) Silent skip with no message:
    The directory the plugin lives in is not on the search
    path.  Confirm with `pn_node_factory_load_plugin` called
    explicitly (e.g. through `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH=$PWD`) that
    the file is loadable in isolation.

If `pn_plugin_init` runs but no node appears in the palette,
double-check that:

  - your `_class_init` set both `category` and `class_name`
    (a NULL `class_name` is what makes the palette skip a
    type), and
  - your `pn_node_factory_register` call succeeded -- the
    function returns FALSE if the type is not a `PnNode`
    descendant or has already been registered.


14. SHIPPING PER-NODE HELP PAGES
--------------------------------

Pipnode's help browser opens a per-node HTML file when the user
right-clicks a palette entry and picks "Help".  The file is named
after the GType name -- for example `PnRtc.html` -- and lives in
the same flat $pkgdatadir/help/ directory whether it ships from the
host (libpipnode) or from a plugin.  The host's
resolver searches several directories in order:

  1. $PIPNODE_HELP_PATH                 (colon-separated dev override)
  2. $XDG_DATA_HOME/pipnode/help/       (per-user installs)
  3. $SRCDIR/data/help/                 (in-tree, host's own files)
  4. $SRCDIR/plugins/<x>/help/          (in-tree, bundled plugins)
  5. $PKGDATADIR/help/                  (system install)

To ship a help file with your plugin:

  - Drop one HTML file per node into your plugin's `help/`
    directory; name each file exactly `<TypeName>.html` (the
    GType name, including the `Pn` prefix).

  - Reference the shared stylesheet with a sibling href --
    `<link rel="stylesheet" href="help.css">` -- so the file
    inherits the host's typography without carrying its own
    copy.  The host installs `help.css` into the same
    directory as your file at install time.

  - Optionally cross-link back to the host index with
    `<a href="help-index.html">` so the user can jump up to
    the table of contents.

  - In your plugin's Makefile.am, ship the files with:

        pnhelpdir = $(pkgdatadir)/help
        pnhelp_DATA = \
            help/<TypeNameA>.html \
            help/<TypeNameB>.html
        EXTRA_DIST = $(pnhelp_DATA)

    and they install alongside the host's help directory.

  - For dev / test runs against the build tree, no extra
    plumbing is needed: the in-tree fallback walks every
    `plugins/<x>/help/` so `make` followed by `src/pipnode`
    finds your help files without `make install`.

A plugin that omits the help file falls back gracefully: the
right-click "Help" entry opens the host's help-index.html
instead of leaving the menu item silent.

The bundled `plugins/network/help/` (PnPing, PnDns) is a worked
example of the shape your own plugin's help/ directory should
take.


15. EXTENDING THE NODE SETTINGS DIALOG (ABI v2)
-----------------------------------------------

The auto-generated property tab the dialog draws for every class in
the node's GObject type chain is enough for any node that is fully
described by plain GObject properties: a string editor for strings,
a check button for booleans, an enum combo for enums, and so on.
Plugin-shipped node types get this for free -- no extra work in the
plugin's `_class_init` is required.

Three things, however, the auto-generated tab cannot do on its own,
and they are the reason for the dialog-extension hooks introduced
in ABI v2:

  - The right widget for one of your properties is not the default.
    Examples: a serial-port path that should pick from a sysfs walk
    instead of accepting any string; a file path that should open a
    file chooser; a numeric field whose valid range depends on
    another property; a read-only label for a value the node writes
    itself (a fetched rate, a last-update timestamp).

  - Your configuration is structured rather than scalar -- a list
    of rules, an array of {key, value} pairs, a route table.  The
    auto-generated tab would render the underlying JSON-string
    property as a single giant entry, which is unusable.

  - Configuration needs more than one tab -- a Diagnostics page
    showing live state, a Logs tail, a one-shot "Scan for devices"
    button.

Three optional vfunc slots on `PnNodeClass` cover these.  All three
default to `NULL`; assigning any one of them is opt-in.  The dialog
calls them once per dialog open with the node instance, the
specific GParamSpec (for `build_property_editor`), and the
PnNodeDialog GtkWindow (suitable as the `transient-for` of any
child dialog you spawn).

Helpers exposed for the rows / pages you do NOT want to write from
scratch live in `<pipnode/pn-node-dialog-helpers.h>`:

```c
GtkWidget *pn_node_dialog_new_property_grid (void);
void       pn_node_dialog_attach_row        (GtkGrid *grid, guint row,
                                             const gchar *label,
                                             GtkWidget *editor);
GtkWidget *pn_node_dialog_default_editor    (GObject *target,
                                             GParamSpec *pspec);
void       pn_node_dialog_append_page       (GtkNotebook *notebook,
                                             GtkWidget *page,
                                             const gchar *title);
```

`pn_node_dialog_default_editor` is the same builder the auto-
generated tab uses; calling it from your override delegates one
property back to the host without recursing into your override
again.

15.1 build_property_editor: take over one row
---------------------------------------------

```c
GtkWidget *(*build_property_editor) (PnNode      *self,
                                     GParamSpec  *pspec,
                                     GObject     *target,
                                     GtkWindow   *dialog_parent);
```

Called once for every writable property the dialog renders for
this class.  Return a #GtkWidget bound to @target via the standard
GObject machinery (`g_object_bind_property`,
`g_signal_connect_object`) to take the row over; return %NULL to
let the host's default editor build the row.  Use
`g_signal_connect_object (target, "notify::other_prop", cb, your_widget, 0)`
when the editor needs to react to changes on a sibling property
-- the handler auto-disconnects when the dialog closes and your
widget is destroyed.

Worked example -- a serial-port combo populated from a live
sysfs walk:

```c
static GtkWidget *
my_node_build_property_editor (PnNode      *self,
                               GParamSpec  *pspec,
                               GObject     *target,
                               GtkWindow   *parent)
{
    if (g_strcmp0 (pspec->name, "device") != 0)
        return NULL;                                /* host default */

    GtkWidget *combo = gtk_combo_box_text_new ();
    gchar **devices = my_node_list_devices ();      /* sysfs walk */

    gtk_combo_box_text_append (GTK_COMBO_BOX_TEXT (combo),
                               "", "No Device");
    for (gchar **p = devices; p && *p; p++)
        gtk_combo_box_text_append (GTK_COMBO_BOX_TEXT (combo),
                                   *p, *p);
    g_strfreev (devices);

    gtk_widget_set_hexpand (combo, TRUE);
    g_object_bind_property (target, "device", combo, "active-id",
                            G_BINDING_SYNC_CREATE
                            | G_BINDING_BIDIRECTIONAL);
    return combo;
}
```

Wire it up in `_class_init`:

```c
node_class->build_property_editor = my_node_build_property_editor;
```

15.2 build_class_tab: replace the whole property tab
----------------------------------------------------

```c
GtkWidget *(*build_class_tab) (PnNode    *self,
                               GtkWindow *dialog_parent);
```

Called once instead of the introspection-driven default.  Return
the #GtkWidget that should sit in this class's notebook tab;
return %NULL to fall back to the default.  Use this when the
configuration is a list / table / graph editor that does not
naturally decompose into one widget per GObject property -- the
in-tree #PnFilter and #PnSet rule editors are the worked
examples.  When you want the host's grid layout for some rows
and your own widget for others, build a
`pn_node_dialog_new_property_grid()` and attach via
`pn_node_dialog_attach_row()` so your tab matches the look of
its siblings.

15.3 build_extra_pages: append additional notebook pages
--------------------------------------------------------

```c
void (*build_extra_pages) (PnNode      *self,
                           GtkNotebook *notebook,
                           GtkWindow   *dialog_parent);
```

Called once after the per-class property tabs are populated,
walked in chain order so the leaf class's pages land last.
Implementations call `pn_node_dialog_append_page (notebook, page,
"Title")` for each extra page.  Use for content that is not
naturally a property form: a Diagnostics page showing live signal
strength, a Logs tail, a "Scan for devices" button.

15.4 The free `busy` overlay
----------------------------

If your node exposes a read-only boolean property named `busy`,
the dialog will automatically:

  - watch it via `notify::busy`;
  - centre a spinner with the label "Initializing device…" over
    the entire notebook while the property is `TRUE`;
  - lock every page in the notebook from interaction during that
    window.

This is implemented in the host with `g_object_class_find_property
(klass, "busy")` -- no opt-in vfunc, no helper to call.  Plugin
node types that need to lock their settings dialog while a worker
thread runs an async device handshake just need to install the
property; everything else falls into place.

15.5 ABI version requirement
----------------------------

Using any of the three vfuncs requires `PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION = 2`.
A plugin built against ABI v1 can keep loading against an ABI v2
host (the host's loader matches on the version field), but the
v1 plugin's `PnNodeClass` struct does not carry the new vfunc
slots so the dialog falls back to the host default for every
property -- exactly the v1 behaviour.  Plugins compiled against
ABI v2 refuse to load against an ABI v1 host (the version check
catches the mismatch up-front and surfaces a clear `GError`).


