17. QUICK CHECKLIST
-------------------

Before publishing a plugin, walk through this list:

  [ ] One C file (or several, your call) defining your
      `PnNode` subclasses with `G_DEFINE_TYPE`.
  [ ] `_class_init` sets `class_name`, `icon`, `color`,
      `category`, `has_input`, `has_output`, and `receive`.
  [ ] One translation unit exporting `pn_plugin_init` with
      `G_MODULE_EXPORT`.
  [ ] `PnPluginInfo` has `abi_version = PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION`
      and a non-empty `name` and `version`.
  [ ] Each node type your plugin provides is registered with
      `pn_node_factory_register` from `pn_plugin_init`.
  [ ] Built as a `.so` (no `lib` prefix, no version suffix)
      that resolves with `ldd` and exposes `pn_plugin_init`
      under `nm -D`.
  [ ] Installed into one of the directories from section 7,
      typically `~/.local/share/pipnode/plugins/` for an
      end-user install.
  [ ] Verified with `G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all pipnode` that the
      loader logs `loaded plugin "<name>" <version>`.
  [ ] Verified that the new node appears in the palette and
      can be saved into a worksheet, closed, and reloaded.
  [ ] One `help/<TypeName>.html` file per registered node type,
      installed via your Makefile.am into `$(pkgdatadir)/help/`
      (see section 14).  Optional but strongly recommended:
      without it the palette's right-click Help falls back to
      the host index.

If every box is ticked, you are done.


18. PLUGIN LICENSING AND THE PLUGIN EXCEPTION

The pipnode core (the `src/` application and the `lib/` host library)
is licensed under the GNU GPL version 3 or later.  Because a plugin
links against `libpipnode` and uses pipnode's types and functions, a
plugin would normally be a derivative work of the core and would
therefore have to be GPL as well.

Pipnode grants an explicit exception so that this is NOT the case.
The file `LICENSE.PLUGIN-EXCEPTION` (an additional permission under
GPL v3 section 7) allows any plugin that talks to pipnode only through
the documented plugin interface to be released under ANY license,
including a proprietary one.  In short:

  - You may publish your plugin under any terms you like -- GPL,
    MIT, a commercial EULA, or no public license at all (a private
    binary).  This applies to everyone, not just the pipnode author.

  - The exception covers whatever your plugin extends: node types,
    and any future plugin kinds documented here (for example
    worksheet storage / serialization back-ends or export formats).

  - The condition is that the plugin interacts with pipnode SOLELY
    through the plugin interface described in this file -- the
    `pn_plugin_init` entry point and the public functions and types
    in the installed pipnode headers (`pn-plugin.h`, `pn-node.h`,
    `pn-node-factory.h`, `pn-message.h`, and the others).  Do not
    reach into private struct internals to stay clearly within the
    exception.

  - If instead you MODIFY the pipnode core itself (anything in
    `src/` or `lib/`), those modifications are still governed by the
    GPL.  Adding a new host-side extension point is a core change and
    is therefore GPL; the plugin that uses it can still be
    proprietary.

This is why the project can ship some plugins as paid, sponsor-only
binaries while keeping the core fully open, and why you are free to do
the same with your own plugins.  See the top-level `LICENSE`,
`COPYING`, and `LICENSE.PLUGIN-EXCEPTION` for the exact terms.
